+*   **Memory Complexity:** Significantly reduced memory consumption compared to standard 3DGS training because the initial feed-forward Gaussians are redundant and are pruned during fine-tuning [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Achieves photorealism on par with or surpassing other methods, particularly in urban view synthesis [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Fast convergence, reduced memory footprint, ability to handle large-scale scenes with sparse initial points [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the initial feed-forward prediction; if the initialization is poor, fine-tuning may struggle to correct global structure errors [G].
+*   **When to use:** Large-scale scenes (e.g., urban environments) where SfM point clouds are sparse or noisy, and computational resources for long training times are limited [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** Small-scale scenes with dense, high-quality SfM points where standard 3DGS training is sufficient and fast enough [G].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of fine-tuning 3D Gaussian Splatting emerged as a strategy to leverage pretrained models or efficient initialization schemes to overcome the slow convergence and high memory costs of standard 3DGS training. EVolSplat introduced a method where a feed-forward process predicts initial Gaussians, which are then fine-tuned using standard 3DGS optimization, enabling efficient volume-based Gaussian splatting for urban view synthesis [S1]. This approach highlights the benefit of decoupling initialization from optimization to manage the trade-off between reconstruction quality and computational resources [S1].
+Fine-tuning strategies for 3D Gaussian Splatting have emerged to address the limitations of training from random or sparse initialization, particularly in large-scale and urban environments. The method was notably applied in EVolSplat, which introduced a feed-forward initialization step followed by differentiable optimization with densification control [S1]. This approach leverages the speed of feed-forward networks to generate a coarse representation, which is then refined using the differentiable rendering pipeline of 3DGS [S1]. The technique is part of a broader trend in differentiable volume rendering to decouple initialization from optimization to improve efficiency and quality [S1].
 
